    tmk:authoringReasoning "Typed in the same judgement as the concept: The Regulations expressly define the term, making it a distinct legal concept best classified as a registration record. It is broader than GC-0113, international registration designating Australia, because its definition does not require an Australian designation. The Manual also distinguishes international registration from Australian protection by describing the effect of cancellation on a protected international trade mark. The defining legislative passage is regulation 17A.2. The only reference supplied under PROVISIONS, regulation 17A.27, is described in the Manual as defining a declaration period for limitations, so it is not listed as a legislative basis for this concept."@en-AU ;
    tmk:conceptGroup "instrument_or_record" ;
    tmk:confidence 9.7e-01 ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Confirm the relationship to GC-0113 and GC-0114 against their full definitions; only their labels are supplied."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GT-0150" ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMR1995/r17A.2/international-registration> .

tmka:GT-0151 tmk:authoredBy "gpt-6.1-sol" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Typed in the same judgement as the concept: Section 6 expressly defines the term. The Manual distinguishes the old register under the 1955 Act from the Register under the 1995 Act and describes the transfer of disclaimer particulars between them. This supports a distinct historical record concept, rather than a duplicate of the general Register of Trade Marks concept. The cited sections 207 and 238 concern that transition as described by the Manual; the other supplied provision references concern disclaimers or examination rather than defining the old register."@en-AU ;
    tmk:conceptGroup "instrument_or_record" ;
    tmk:confidence 9.7e-01 ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Confirm the relationship to GC-0089 and verify sections 207 and 238 directly; their transitional effects are described here only through the Manual passages."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GT-0151" ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/old-register> .

tmka:GT-0152 tmk:authoredBy "gpt-6.1-sol" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Typed in the same judgement as the concept: Section 6 expressly defines the expression by reference to a person's trade activities. It is therefore a legal attribution concept, not merely a services category or generic phrase, and no existing concept duplicates it. The historical section 41 extracts illustrate its role in distinguishing an applicant's services from those of other persons. Subject_matter fits best because the expression identifies the services to which trade mark protection relates. Sections 8 and 7(3) are mentioned in nearby notes about authorised use, but the supplied passages do not establish them as grounds for this definition."@en-AU ;
    tmk:conceptGroup "subject_matter" ;
    tmk:confidence 9.4e-01 ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Check whether subject_matter is the preferred grouping for statutory attribution concepts; the supplied section 41 extracts concern the version prior to Raising the Bar, not necessarily the current provision."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GT-0152" ;
    tmk:origin "authored" ;
    tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" ;
    tmk:sourcePassage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s6/services-of-a-person> .

tmkc:GC-0054 a skos:Concept,
        tmk:GroundOfRefusal,
        tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "The Manual records that there are currently no regulations made for the purposes of section 18, so this ground is never raised in examination. The concept is real in law and inert in practice, and that gap is the most useful thing about the record."@en-AU ;
    skos:altLabel "prohibited signs"@en-AU,
        "sign that is not to be used as a trade mark"@en-AU ;
    skos:broader tmkc:GC-0073 ;
    skos:inScheme tmkc:scheme-s43 ;
    skos:prefLabel "prohibited sign"@en-AU ;
    tmk:authoredBy "claude-opus-5" ;
    tmk:authoredDate "2026-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    tmk:authoringBasis "corpus_explicit" ;
    tmk:authoringReasoning "Section 39(1) names the class by reference to regulations under section 18, and the Manual states that no such regulations exist. Both halves are explicit, so the concept is recorded with its inertness attached rather than as a live ground."@en-AU ;
    tmk:confidence 8.5e-01 ;
    tmk:definitionSource <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part31/1%231> ;
    tmk:expertShouldCheck "Whether 'prohibited sign' is the right preferred label. The Manual's Part 31 title pairs it with 'prescribed', and the Act itself uses neither word for s 39(1)."@en-AU ;
    tmk:goldRecord "GC-0054" ;
